<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco I. Madero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco I. Madero was a Mexican landowner and politician who challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> in the 1910 election and, after his arrest, called for revolt in the Plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. He became president in 1911 after Díaz resigned. In February 1913 he was overthrown by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> and murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-i-madero.jpg" alt="Francisco I. Madero" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_I_Madero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francisco Ignacio Madero González was born on 30 October 1873 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> of El Rosario in Parras de la Fuente, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a>, into one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s wealthiest families. His grandfather Evaristo Madero Elizondo built a large fortune, in part by shipping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> to Mexican ports during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. Between 1886 and 1892 Francisco was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the Lycée Hoche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, the École des Hautes Études Commerciales in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley. In Paris he took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiritism">Spiritism</a> and came to believe he was a medium. Returning in 1893, he managed a family hacienda at San Pedro, where he installed irrigation and built schools, hospitals and a soap factory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Bernardo Reyes violently crushed a demonstration on 2 April 1903, Madero, who believed his dead brother's spirit was advising him, decided to enter politics. He founded the Benito Juárez Democratic Club, lost a municipal election in 1904 and started opposition newspapers. His 1908 book The Presidential Succession in 1910 urged voters to stop Díaz's reelection, and he funded the Anti-Reelectionist Party. Díaz had said in 1908 that the 1910 election would be free. The regime arrested Madero on 6 June 1910, and about 5,000 other members of the movement were jailed. A fraudulent election on 21 June gave Díaz a huge majority.</p><p>Madero fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and issued the Plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, which declared the election void and called for revolt on 20 November 1910. It also promised the return of land to villages and Indian communities. After a defeat at Casas Grandes he gave up military command, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancho-villa">Pancho Villa</a> and Pascual Orozco took Ciudad Juárez against his orders. Díaz resigned on 25 May 1911 under the Treaty of Ciudad Juárez. Madero kept the Federal Army and demobilised the revolutionaries. Elected in a landslide, he was sworn in as president on 6 November 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His slowness on land reform led Emiliano Zapata to proclaim the Plan of Ayala on 25 November 1911, and Orozco rebelled in 1912. Conservatives opposed him, and the press he had freed criticised him. In February 1913 Félix Díaz, Bernardo Reyes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> led a coup, supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, during the Ten Tragic Days of 9 to 19 February. Huerta switched sides, and Madero was arrested and forced to resign. He and Vice President José María Pino Suárez were murdered, and his brother Gustavo A. Madero was kidnapped, tortured and killed.</p><p>His death united revolutionaries against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a>. Venustiano Carranza, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutionalist-army">Constitutionalist Army</a>. He was known as the Apostle of Democracy, and the Monument to the Revolution, completed in 1938, holds his remains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco I. Madero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolution that began in November 1910 under <b>Francisco I. Madero</b> destroyed the old federal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parras de la Fuente, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.44,-102.18&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Parras de la Fuente on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francisco I. Madero"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4066 5340h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4066 5340h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.44,-102.18&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
